{
  "0": "Across all four datasets, replace raw GRU-state readout with causal recency pooling: normalized weights 0.8^(t-j) over nonpadding positions j<=t. Apply at every supervised position and read the final real pooled position at inference. Prior gains motivate testing this readout with the incumbent mixed loss. Preserve history truncation, embeddings, GRU topology, scoring, loss and optimizer; keep predictor behavior consistent.",
  "1": "Across all four datasets, increase the incumbent pointwise logistic weight from 0.2 to 0.5: softplus(negative-positive) + 0.5*[softplus(-positive)+softplus(negative)], averaged over existing nonpadding targets. Stronger direct supervision may improve candidate separation after the previous mixed-loss gain. Preserve samples, embeddings, GRU topology, readout, scoring, optimizer and training settings.",
  "2": "Across all four datasets, add a deterministic training-frequency input feature without new learned parameters: multiply each history item's existing embedding by clamp((median_positive_count/count(item))^0.25, 0.5, 2.0), using counts from training interactions only. This may reduce domination by ubiquitous history items. Keep target/candidate embeddings, GRU topology, readout, scoring, loss and optimizer fixed. Save the scales with each single model and apply identically at inference; retain zero padding.",
  "3": "Across all four datasets, jointly set hidden/embedding width to 256, dropout to 0.2, learning rate to 0.0007 and maximum history to 64. Greater recurrent capacity may capture richer interests, while stronger dropout and a smaller step constrain overfitting; shorter histories reduce computation and retain most observed sequences. Preserve normalized tied embeddings, GRU depth, readout, scoring and loss. Retain seed 42, 200 batches/epoch and the 200-epoch cap; save dimensions and truncation for predictor consistency."
}
